{"name":"Pewter Patter","hex":"#bab4a6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pewter","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An alloy of approximately 93–98% tin and 1–2% copper, and the balance of antimony.","An alloy of tin and lead."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dark, dull grey colour, like that of the metal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pewter"},{"term":"patter","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A soft repeated sound, as of rain falling, or feet walking on a hard surface."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make irregularly repeated sounds of low-to-moderate magnitude and lower-than-average pitch.","To spatter; to sprinkle."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/patter"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pewter","blocks":[{"text":"Pewter is a malleable metal alloy consisting of tin (85–99%), antimony, copper (2%), bismuth, and sometimes silver. In the past, it was an alloy of tin and as much as 40% lead, but most modern pewter, in order to prevent lead poisoning, is not made with lead. Pewter has a low melting point, around 170–230 °C (338–446 °F), depending on the exact mixture of metals. The word pewter is possibly a variation of \"spelter\", a term for zinc alloys."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pewter","wikidata":"Q852372"},{"title":"Patter","blocks":[{"text":"Patter is a prepared and practiced speech that is designed to produce a desired response from its audience. Examples of occupations with a patter include the auctioneer, salesperson, dance caller, magician, and comedian."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patter","wikidata":"Q7148351"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Fork-Viking-Pewter-detail.jpg/250px-Fork-Viking-Pewter-detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Fork-Viking-Pewter-detail.jpg/250px-Fork-Viking-Pe